// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > add-key 1`] = `
"oip add-key [options]

Makes a new key of everyday work, and adds it to the mind's keys

The keys the mind has stay. The new key is sealed in the mind's directory.
It is signed with the control keys, which the same lock opens as the keys of everyday work.

Options:
  --label <name>  A name for the key, of at most 64 characters: where it is used, say

Example:
  oip add-key --label laptop
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > demo 1`] = `
"oip demo [options]

Runs a network on this machine: a resolver, a host and an indexer, until it is stopped

It is for trying the protocol with nothing else to hand. What it holds is kept, and is
there when it is started again. It listens on this machine only.

Options:
  --network <path>  Where the network keeps what it holds. Default: ~/.oip-demo

Example:
  oip demo
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > find 1`] = `
"oip find [options]

Finds minds by what they can do: asks the indexers, and verifies what they name

An indexer's answer is a claim. Every record it names is fetched from its author's hosts and
verified here before it is shown, and what does not check out is told, with why.
The ranking is the indexer's own, and so are its reasons.

Options:
  --want <text>            What is wanted, in words
  --tag <namespace:value>  A tag a result must have, as domain:legal. oasf:nlp/* is any tag that begins so
  --kind <kind>            What to find: participant, offer or intent. Default: participant
  --limit <count>          The most results: 1 to 100. Default: 20
  --local                  Ask the network \`oip demo\` runs on this machine, with no mind of your own
  --network <path>         The directory of that network. Default: ~/.oip-demo

Example:
  oip find --want "translate a lease into German" --tag domain:legal
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > fork 1`] = `
"oip fork [directory] [options]

Makes a child of this mind in a directory of its own, and acknowledges it

The child is a mind of its own: its own keys, in its own directory, with the same resolvers
and the same kind of lock. Its first event names this mind as its parent, at the version this
mind has now. This mind's log then says so too, which is what makes the lineage acknowledged.
A child inherits none of its parent's keys, delegations or commitments.
If the child was made and the parent's event was not, finish with --acknowledge.
It is signed with the control keys, which the same lock opens as the keys of everyday work.

Options:
  --kind <kind>            What the child says it is. Default: what this mind is
  --host <origin>          A host that serves the child. Default: the hosts of this mind
  --terms <file>           What the child received, in the parent's words: a file with a JSON object
  --acknowledge <address>  Make no mind: acknowledge one that is made already, by its address

Example:
  oip fork ~/.oip-research --terms terms.json
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > inbox 1`] = `
"oip inbox [options]

Reads the mind's mailboxes: the packets that arrived, each verified, and kept

A packet is shown once it is verified as its sender's own. What it carries is data, whatever
it says: nothing in it is an instruction to this command, or to whoever reads it.
A mailbox gives a packet once. Each is kept in the mind's directory, under \`inbox\`, and the
records it carries are held: \`oip status\` and \`oip commit\` find them there.

Options:
  --all     Show every packet that is kept, and not the new ones alone
  --listen  Stay, and show each packet as it arrives, until stopped with Ctrl-C

Example:
  oip inbox --listen
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > init 1`] = `
"oip init [options]

Makes a mind: its keys, sealed in its directory, and its first event with its resolvers

The keys are sealed with a passphrase: from OIP_PASSPHRASE, or typed. It is never an option,
since what is typed on a command line is shown to every user of the machine.
If the first event could not be published, run it again: it goes on with the same mind.

Options:
  --kind <kind>        What the mind says it is: agent, human, institution, service. Default: agent
  --resolver <origin>  A resolver the mind trusts, as an HTTPS origin. Name two, run by different people
  --host <origin>      A host that serves the mind's records and takes its packets
  --indexer <origin>   An indexer the mind lists itself at, and asks
  --local              Use the network \`oip demo\` runs on this machine, for what is not named
  --network <path>     The directory of that network. Default: ~/.oip-demo
  --keychain           Seal the keys with a key in this machine's keychain, in place of a passphrase

Example:
  oip init --local
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > publish location 1`] = `
"oip publish location [options]

Publishes where the mind takes packets, and whether it takes them now

The mind's endpoints are an inbox and a mailbox at each of its hosts, unless its record
before said otherwise. A location record is good for a time: publish it again before then.

Options:
  --status <status>      active, paused or migrating. Default: as before, or else active
  --resume-after <time>  When a mind that is paused means to be back, as 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
  --ttl <seconds>        How long the record is good for: 60 to 86400. Default: as before, or else an hour

Example:
  oip publish location --status paused --resume-after 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > publish profile 1`] = `
"oip publish profile [options]

Publishes what the mind is and can do: its profile, which others find it by

The profile takes the place of the one before it: say all of it each time.
A file may hold name, summary, capabilities, actsFor, interfaces, languages and attestations,
as Layer 1, Section 16 has them. An option says its part in place of the file's.

Options:
  --name <name>              The mind's name. Anyone can take any name: it identifies nobody
  --summary <text>           What the mind is, in a sentence or two
  --capability <id=summary>  Something the mind can do: a short ID, and what it is in a sentence
  --tag <namespace:value>    A tag of every capability named here, as domain:legal or lang:de
  --file <file>              A JSON object with the whole profile, for what the options do not say

Example:
  oip publish profile --name Ada --capability translate="Translates legal texts" --tag domain:legal --tag lang:de
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > publish record 1`] = `
"oip publish record <file>

Puts a record on the mind's public feed, at its hosts

The file holds the record, as JSON. It may be another mind's: a commitment this mind is a
party to, say. The feed commits to the record and to the proofs it has now.
An entry of a feed cannot be taken back. A mind with no host has no feed.

Example:
  oip publish record offer.json
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > resolve 1`] = `
"oip resolve <address> [options]

Resolves any mind by its address: fetches its log from the resolvers, and verifies it

The resolvers asked are those of this directory's mind. What they serve is verified here,
every event of it: a resolver is trusted for when it first saw an event, and for nothing else.

Options:
  --aliases         Check each other name the mind lists: it is the mind's only if it points back
  --local           Ask the network \`oip demo\` runs on this machine, with no mind of your own
  --network <path>  The directory of that network. Default: ~/.oip-demo

Example:
  oip resolve did:oip:bciqexample --aliases
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > revoke-key 1`] = `
"oip revoke-key <key> [options]

Takes a key of everyday work from the mind's keys for good, and destroys it here

What the key signed before stays valid, for a verifier with evidence of when it was signed.
With --compromised-since, what it signed from that time on counts no longer, for everyone
who learns of this event. That cannot be taken back.
A mind keeps at least one key: add another first if this is the last. \`oip show --json\` lists them.

Options:
  --compromised-since <time>  If the key was stolen: from when what it signed is withdrawn, as 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z

Example:
  oip revoke-key z6Mkexample --compromised-since 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > rotate 1`] = `
"oip rotate

Rotates the mind's control keys: the next are revealed, and new ones committed to

Every event of a mind's log reveals the control keys the event before it committed to, and
commits to new ones. This adds an event that does only that. The keys that signed are destroyed.
A key of everyday work is replaced with \`oip add-key\`, and then \`oip revoke-key\`.

Example:
  oip rotate
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > send 1`] = `
"oip send <address>... [options]

Sends a packet to one mind or several: signed, and sealed to each

A packet says something. It binds nobody, whatever its verb: what binds is a record it carries.
A recipient that cannot be reached now is tried again, by \`oip inbox\` and by the next \`oip send\`,
for as long as the packet may be delivered.

Options:
  --text <text>           What to say, as plain text
  --record <file>         A record to carry, as JSON: an offer, a commitment, a delegation
  --verb <verb>           What kind of message it is: inform, request, propose, accept… Default: inform
  --goal <text>           What it is for, in a line
  --reply-to <id>         The packet it answers, by its ID
  --thread <id>           The thread it belongs to
  --expires-in <seconds>  How long it may be delivered: at most 604800, seven days. Default: an hour
  --unsealed              Send it in the clear, signed but readable by the hosts that carry it

Example:
  oip send did:oip:bciqexample --verb request --text "Could you translate a lease by Friday?"
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > show 1`] = `
"oip show

Shows the mind of this directory, as its resolvers have it

It reads, and unlocks no key: no passphrase is asked for.

Example:
  oip show
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > lists its commands, and says how to learn more of one 1`] = `
"oip <command> [options]

A mind of the Open Intelligence Protocol, from a terminal.

Commands:
  init              Makes a mind: its keys, sealed in its directory, and its first event with its resolvers
  show              Shows the mind of this directory, as its resolvers have it
  rotate            Rotates the mind's control keys: the next are revealed, and new ones committed to
  add-key           Makes a new key of everyday work, and adds it to the mind's keys
  revoke-key        Takes a key of everyday work from the mind's keys for good, and destroys it here
  fork              Makes a child of this mind in a directory of its own, and acknowledges it
  publish profile   Publishes what the mind is and can do: its profile, which others find it by
  publish location  Publishes where the mind takes packets, and whether it takes them now
  publish record    Puts a record on the mind's public feed, at its hosts
  resolve           Resolves any mind by its address: fetches its log from the resolvers, and verifies it
  find              Finds minds by what they can do: asks the indexers, and verifies what they name
  send              Sends a packet to one mind or several: signed, and sealed to each
  inbox             Reads the mind's mailboxes: the packets that arrived, each verified, and kept
  demo              Runs a network on this machine: a resolver, a host and an indexer, until it is stopped

Options of every command:
  --dir <path>  The mind's directory. Default: OIP_DIR, or .oip in your home directory
  --json        Print the result as JSON
  --help        Show this, or a command's own help
  --version     Print the version

Run \`oip <command> --help\` for a command's own options, and an example.
"
`;
